const GUIDE = [
 ["Where we're staying",`<ul><li><b>Tokyo:</b> Shinjuku Warm House, Higashi-Shinjuku (<a href="map.html?city=tokyo">map</a>).</li><li><b>Hakone:</b> FUMI, a forest house with a private outdoor bath (<a href="map.html?city=hakone">map</a>).</li><li><b>Kyoto:</b> Kyoto Birch House Spring, by Tofukuji station (<a href="map.html?city=kyoto">map</a>).</li><li><b>Osaka:</b> FDS Aura, near Nagahoribashi station (<a href="map.html?city=osaka">map</a>).</li><li>Check-in times, rooms and messages for the hosts are on the Airbnbs tab.</li><li>All four are self check-in with no front desk, so nobody holds bags before check-in: use station coin lockers.</li></ul>`],
 ["Getting around",`<ul><li><b>Don't buy a JR Pass.</b> Even with Hiroshima, our long-distance trains total ~¥49,000 across 18 days; a 7-day pass is ¥50,000 and doesn't cover the Nozomi.</li><li><b>Suica</b> in your phone wallet works on every train, bus and convenience store, including Kansai.</li><li><b>Hakone Freepass</b> from Shinjuku (2 days, ¥7,100) + Romancecar ~¥1,200.</li><li><b>Shinkansen</b> Odawara → Kyoto and Kyoto ⇄ Hiroshima on the SmartEX app. E seat for Fuji.</li><li><b>Hakone:</b> the Freepass covers the Tozan train, cable car, ropeway, pirate ship and buses, including the bus to Odawara on Day 9.</li><li><b>Hiroshima:</b> Suica works on the trams. The JR ferry to Miyajima is ¥200 plus Miyajima's ¥100 visitor tax.</li><li><b>Osaka Amazing Pass</b> (¥3,500): only worth it on Day 15 if you go up Tsutenkaku. Umeda Sky is free with it only before 15:00.</li><li>Direct <b>Hikari</b> trains from Odawara to Kyoto run every two hours. JR changes its timetables in mid-March, so trust SmartEX over the times here.</li><li>Kyoto buses are slow. Walk, take the subway, or split a taxi (¥1,500–2,500).</li></ul>`],
 ["Luggage forwarding",`<ul><li><b>Day 7 night:</b> send cases Tokyo → the Kyoto house. Overnight bag only for the one Hakone night.</li><li>Kyoto is self check-in: ask the host whether they can receive the cases, or book a Yamato delivery slot for the evening of Mon 15.</li><li><b>Day 13 night:</b> send cases Kyoto → Osaka from a convenience store near Tofukuji (ask the host which one).</li><li>Osaka is self check-in too: ask the FDS Aura host about receiving the cases, or book a Yamato evening slot for Sat 20.</li><li>None of our stays has a front desk, so send from a 7-Eleven or FamilyMart counter (Yamato). ~¥2,000–2,500 per case, arrives next day. Have the destination address and the host’s phone number ready.</li><li>Coin lockers everywhere, ¥400–700.</li></ul>`],
 ["Shopping map",`<dl class="kv"><dt>Retro games</dt><dd>Super Potato (Akiba + Den Den Town), Surugaya, Mandarake, Hard-Off junk bins</dd><dt>Nintendo</dt><dd>Nintendo TOKYO (Shibuya PARCO 6F), Nintendo Museum shop, Nintendo OSAKA (Daimaru Umeda)</dd><dt>Pokémon</dt><dd>MEGA TOKYO (Ikebukuro), Shibuya PARCO, Kyoto Takashimaya, Osaka Daimaru Umeda</dd><dt>TCG</dt><dd>Hareruya 2, Yellow Submarine, Card Rush, Daiya (Nakano), Den Den Town</dd><dt>Knives</dt><dd>Kappabashi, Tsukiji, Aritsugu (Nishiki)</dd><dt>Matcha</dt><dd>Uji</dd><dt>Everything at 2am</dt><dd>Don Quijote</dd></dl><p>Browse in Akihabara, bulk-buy in Den Den Town on Day 16: often cheaper, and no carrying it across the country.</p>`],
 ["Food",`<ul><li><b>Tokyo:</b> Fuunji tsukemen, Afuri ramen, Butagumi tonkatsu, Gyukatsu Motomura, Uobei conveyor sushi, Ichiran.</li><li><b>Hakone:</b> Owakudani black eggs, soba in Gora, amazake at the teahouse.</li><li><b>Kyoto:</b> yudofu, obanzai, Katsukura tonkatsu, Nishiki street food, matcha everything in Uji.</li><li><b>Hiroshima & Miyajima:</b> Hiroshima-style okonomiyaki (layered, with noodles), grilled oysters, fried momiji manju, anago-meshi ekiben.</li><li><b>Osaka:</b> takoyaki, okonomiyaki, kushikatsu, kitsune udon, chanko nabe on sumo day.</li><li>In March: oden from convenience stores and hot canned coffee from vending machines.</li><li>Slurp. Never tip. Say <i>gochisousama deshita</i> as you leave.</li></ul>`],
 ["Money",`<ul><li>Cards widely accepted; carry ¥10,000–20,000 cash for small places and temples.</li><li>Always pay in <b>yen</b>, never GBP.</li><li>7-Eleven and Japan Post ATMs take foreign cards. Don't buy yen in the UK.</li><li>No tipping.</li></ul>`],
 ["Tax-free shopping (refund system since Nov 2026)",`<ul><li><b>In the shop:</b> show your passport, pay the full price including tax, and register how you want the refund paid (usually back to a card). Ask at the till; it varies by shop.</li><li><b>At Kansai, before you check your bags:</b> scan your passport at a customs tax-refund kiosk in the departure hall. Green: you're done. Red: take the goods to the customs counter.</li><li>So keep tax-free purchases where you can get at them until the kiosk is done. If you get a red after checking bags, the airline may not fetch them back.</li><li>Refunds work per receipt: if one item on a receipt has been used, opened (food, cosmetics, medicine) or given away, the whole receipt's refund is lost.</li><li>Keep every receipt together, from Tokyo onwards. Customs must clear the goods within 90 days of purchase.</li><li>Typically ¥5,000 minimum per shop per day. Allow an extra 45–60 minutes at the airport.</li><li>Knives go in checked bags, after the kiosk.</li></ul>`],
 ["Hiroshima & Miyajima",`<ul><li><b>Tides:</b> check a Miyajima tide table. High tide and the torii floats; low tide and you can walk out to it. Either is good; mid-afternoon high tide is ideal.</li><li><b>Peace Museum:</b> quiet and confronting. Allow the full 1h30; the main hall is the core.</li><li><b>Deer:</b> feeding them is banned on Miyajima (unlike Nara). They will still eat your map.</li><li>The ropeway stops early (~16:00 last up in March). If you miss it, Daisho-in is free and just as memorable.</li></ul>`],
 ["If it rains",`<ul><li><b>Fuji day (Day 7):</b> it's only worth going if the mountain is visible. Check the forecast on Day 5: Day 6 is all indoors, so the two swap (change the bus tickets first). If both days are bad, stay in Tokyo: teamLab Borderless at Azabudai Hills, Odaiba (Miraikan, the Gundam, Joypolis arcade), or a second Akihabara and Nakano round.</li><li><b>Tokyo generally:</b> Day 3's Shibuya half is mostly indoors; do Gyoen and Meiji Jingu on whichever morning is dry.</li><li><b>Hakone (Day 8):</b> the ropeway stops in high wind or when volcanic gas is high, and a replacement bus runs. Swap Owakudani for the Pola Museum of Art or the Okada Museum; the Open-Air Museum has indoor galleries.</li><li><b>Hiroshima day:</b> the museum is indoors and Miyajima still works under an umbrella. Skip the ropeway in cloud.</li><li><b>Kyoto:</b> the bamboo grove and Fushimi Inari are good in light rain. In heavy rain swap Arashiyama for Nijo Castle, the Kyoto Railway Museum, the Manga Museum and Nishiki's covered arcades.</li><li><b>Nara:</b> Todai-ji's hall is indoors; the deer don't mind.</li><li><b>Osaka:</b> Kaiyukan aquarium, the Cup Noodles Museum in Ikeda, Round1 Stadium, and the covered arcades from Shinsaibashi to Namba. USJ runs in rain and queues are shorter.</li><li>Every convenience store sells a ¥700 clear umbrella.</li></ul>`],
 ["Using this site offline",`<ul><li>After you've opened the site once with signal, the itinerary, lists, guide and Airbnb details all work with no connection.</li><li>Maps: open each of the four maps once and zoom into the areas you care about. The areas you've looked at are saved; areas you haven't will be blank offline.</li><li>"Map" and "Directions" links open Google Maps, which needs data. In Google Maps you can also download each city as an offline area.</li><li>On iPhone: Share → Add to Home Screen. On Android: menu → Add to Home screen. It then opens like an app.</li><li>Ticks and the spending log live on each phone separately; they don't sync between us.</li></ul>`],
 ["Private onsen tips",`<ul><li>Shower and rinse before getting in; the bath is for soaking, not washing.</li><li>It’s hot (~40–42°C). Ten minutes at a time, drink water, no soaking straight after drinking.</li><li>With a private bath there’s no etiquette worry about tattoos or swimwear.</li></ul>`],
 ["If something goes wrong",`<ul><li><b>110</b> police, <b>119</b> ambulance and fire.</li><li><b>Japan Visitor Hotline</b> (JNTO, 24h, English): 050-3816-2787.</li><li>Install the <b>Safety tips</b> app for earthquake and weather alerts in English.</li><li>Lost something? Every station and police box (koban) has a lost-property log; things really do come back.</li><li>Keep the travel insurance emergency number in your phone notes.</li></ul>`],
 ["Etiquette & five words",`<ul><li>Stand left on escalators in Tokyo, right in Osaka.</li><li>No phone calls on trains. Don't eat while walking. Take rubbish with you.</li><li>Shoes off at raised entryways.</li></ul><dl class="kv"><dt>sumimasen</dt><dd>excuse me / sorry</dd><dt>arigatou gozaimasu</dt><dd>thank you</dd><dt>onegaishimasu</dt><dd>please</dd><dt>daijoubu</dt><dd>it's fine / no thanks</dd><dt>oishii</dt><dd>delicious</dd></dl>`],
 ["What's on while we're there",`<ul><li><b>Grand Sumo</b>, Osaka: mid-March to 28 March. We go Sun 21.</li><li><b>Plum blossom</b>: Yushima Tenjin, Kitano Tenmangu, Osaka Castle.</li><li><b>Mon 22 March</b>: national holiday.</li><li><b>USJ Cool Japan 2027</b>: Sailor Moon 4-D to 5 April; Detective Conan World and The Apothecary Diaries to 30 June.</li><li><b>DisneySea:</b> Journey to the Center of the Earth reopens 9 March, the day before we go.</li><li>The Hanatoro lantern walk in Kyoto no longer exists (it last ran in 2022).</li><li>Main cherry blossom usually starts ~20–24 March in Tokyo, ~24–27 in Osaka. We may catch the first trees at the very end.</li></ul>`],
];

